<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>FORGE Lab</title><link>https://forge-blog.github.io/</link><description>Recent content on FORGE Lab</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://forge-blog.github.io/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Lessons from 59 Linux Kernel Bug Reports</title><link>https://forge-blog.github.io/posts/linux-kernel-3-month-lookback/</link><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://forge-blog.github.io/posts/linux-kernel-3-month-lookback/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;At Microsoft&amp;rsquo;s FORGE Lab, we used &lt;a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/12/defense-at-ai-speed-microsofts-new-multi-model-agentic-security-system-tops-leading-industry-benchmark/"&gt;codename MDASH&lt;/a&gt; to scan a subset of Linux kernel subsystems from late May to mid-June 2026. One full-time researcher and one intern led this three-month effort, which has so far produced &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/?q=mid%3A20261013051553.71887-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20261012215703.30592-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20260925024408.32143-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20260925015251.21168-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20260901183151.16648-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20260818164825.63967-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260827051859.45511-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260819145658.29872-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260826154456.85974-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260901182136.13745-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20260901180600.10394-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20260901175834.8557-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20260901174934.6275-1-cenzhang%40linux.microsoft.com+OR+mid%3A20260828041925.36758-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260821182449.79785-2-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260821182449.79785-3-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260820200245.44312-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260820010617.46851-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260816035205.57966-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260825052404.45665-3-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260822045407.28983-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260819014858.76707-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260817013237.2797-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260816173713.28998-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260722193719.81157-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260717210143.4734-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260724164719.99563-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260721143602.64677-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260719232153.1405569-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260706212556.3199234-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260720204430.1886091-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260720223400.1939998-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260720034514.23053-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260723000016.84091-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260722223832.3524006-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260722004818.72310-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260720155210.34229-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260722002951.2614721-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260722002951.2614721-2-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260721232805.2451198-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260720214103.47732-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260719221523.1336739-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260718183420.13301-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3ACAB8m9WgETt0AjmFwE%3DF-CKjGXsK6_WDv0%3DkbYRcC8-noo%2BamnA%40mail.gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260713233230.3553593-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260713221551.3344650-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260713215510.3288580-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260711005955.1467140-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260710230724.942574-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260710014109.536177-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260709220501.395659-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260706232850.3333016-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260707203215.2752-1-blbllhy%40gmail.com+OR+mid%3ACAB8m9Wh559e%2B%3Dn8z51gB8DrbEyCc2mc0MgGjrRR6_VXBmU%3D2AQ%40mail.gmail.com+OR+mid%3A20260707015440.3420673-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260702185805.615241-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260702224519.800135-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260702010516.221830-1-xmei5%40asu.edu+OR+mid%3A20260627024023.2201160-1-xmei5%40asu.edi"&gt;59 public upstream reports&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="cves.txt"&gt;33 assigned CVEs&lt;/a&gt;. We confirmed working root privilege-escalation exploits for eight of those CVEs, and fixes for 38 of our reports have been merged into mainline.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Finding the bugs was only part of the work. The initial scans produced 26,502 raw findings in about two weeks, but turning those results into reproducible bugs, upstream reports, and patches took months of expert investigation and collaboration with Linux maintainers.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Memory Safety Bugs in Rust Standard Library</title><link>https://forge-blog.github.io/posts/rust-std-memory-safety/</link><pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://forge-blog.github.io/posts/rust-std-memory-safety/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Microsoft&amp;rsquo;s FORGE Lab found several memory safety issues in Rust&amp;rsquo;s standard library (&lt;code&gt;std&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;core&lt;/code&gt;), including cases where code written entirely in safe Rust can trigger undefined behavior. The research used Hyenas, FORGE&amp;rsquo;s frontier research version of codename MDASH, an AI-powered vulnerability-scanning system. The findings reveal three recurring patterns of misplaced trust in unsafe code: trusting code not to panic, trusting user-provided implementations to be correct, and trusting the underlying system.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>